      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To completely factor the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the greatest common factor (GCF):**\n   First, we need to find the GCF of the coefficients and the variables in the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\).\n\n   - The coefficient of \\(9x^2\\) is \\(9\\), and the coefficient of \\(3x\\) is \\(3\\). The GCF of \\(9\\) and \\(3\\) is \\(3\\).\n   - Both terms have the variable \\(x\\) with the smallest power being \\(1\\). Therefore, the GCF of the variable part is \\(x^1 = x\\).\n\n   Combining these, the GCF of the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\) is \\(3x\\).\n\n2. **Factor out the GCF:**\n   Next, we factor out \\(3x\\) from each term in the expression.\n\n   - \\(9x^2\\) (where \\(9x^2\\) can be written as \\(3x \\cdot 3x\\))\n   - \\(3x\\) (which is already divisible by \\(3x\\))\n\n   So, we write:\n\n   \\[\n   9x^2 + 3x = 3x(3x) + 3x(1) = 3x(3x + 1)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Combine the results:**\n   The completely factored form of the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\) is:\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{3x(3x + 1)}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{3x(3x + 1)}\\).",
